WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.650
We are looking at the dividing line in modern bio-manufacturing.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.870
On one side, traditional thermal processing applies aggressive heat to clear pathogens.

3
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.510
On the other, continuous flow optics achieved the exact same clearance using light.

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.810
Applying heat reliably neutralizes viruses,

5
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.110
but that exact same thermal energy denatures the delicate three-dimensional structures

6
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.530
of therapeutic proteins.

7
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.730
This physical limitation severely impacts the most advanced biological treatments.

8
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.190
Process engineers face a direct contradiction.

9
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.750
Delivering lethal energy to destroy contaminant risks destroying the therapeutic payload

10
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.050
right along with it.

11
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.890
Until production facilities can destroy pathogens at scale without heat,

12
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.670
manufacturing throughput remains strictly capped by the physical fragility of the product

13
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.190
itself.

14
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.650
To avoid heat, the industry relies on severe chemical and mechanical workarounds, pushing

15
00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:01.250
the product through acidic low pH shifts or forcing it through size-exclusion nanofilters.

16
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.090
These methods introduce their own strict vulnerabilities.

17
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.310
Filters can clog or shear large molecules under pressure,

18
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.350
and chemical shifts require neutralizing buffers,

19
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.030
demanding massive validation efforts for every new batch.

20
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.350
The heaviest penalty is logistical.

21
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.910
Because these athermal steps are difficult to verify during active processing,

22
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.830
facilities must lock highly perishable biological matrices in quarantine for weeks while

23
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.870
waiting for retrospective infectivity assays from the lab.

24
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.830
A short shelf-life cell or plasma product degrading in a holding tank represents massive

25
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.050
financial waste and directly delays critical doses reaching patients.

26
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.390
Instructional Biology has engineered a continuous flow alternative.

27
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.250
By combining impulsive stimulated Raman scattering with a machine learning controller,

28
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:57.510
called ISRS-ML, the system delivers a complete athermal clearance mechanism.

29
00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.030
This shifts quality assurance away from retrospective laboratory delays.

30
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:07.350
The regulatory and commercial value of this tightly controlled physical process lies in

31
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.950
eliminating the post-batch assay hold entirely.

32
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.790
The standard legacy steps include inducing a severe low pH chemical shift,

33
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.650
and physically pushing the fluid through a virus-retentive size-exclusion nanofilter.

34
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:25.210
We insert the ISRS-ML module directly into the center of this cascade,

35
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.550
acting as a uniquely optical and acoustic barrier.

36
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.470
Because the technology is severable, contract manufacturers can plug this specific optical

37
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.290
step directly into existing production chains.

38
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.990
ISRS-ML resolves the central bio-manufacturing paradox.

